    "turn_text": "these numbers are coming in, this is where we're digesting it in as real a time as everybody else. I feel like people would come up to me before the election and they'd say, what's going to happen? What's the inside scoop? And clearly, I don't know if that had much fit inside the scoop. But we did have polling. We had information. We had things that weren't necessarily available to everybody else. Now the numbers, as they're coming in, as they're being counted, we're getting that in the same real time as everybody else, trying to figure out what is the difference between 2020 and now. Is it enthusiasm? Is it simply, you know, even look what happened in Kentucky? A lot of states had it where voting was much, much easier. We had voting open a lot longer in Kentucky. We had mail voting in 2020, if you remember. And then, you know, this year we had kind of a disaster on election day in Jefferson County with the polls being really slow to react. What are the things that happen? How does that manifest across the country? What, what exactly does it mean? That's what we'll be looking into in the coming months. You were about two to one over your opponent, though. Did you know right away right at seven o'clock",
